---
import RootLayout from '../layouts/RootLayout.astro';
import { metricLabels, overallWeights } from '../data/config';
import { models } from '../data/models';

const totalModelsCount = models.length;
const uniqueProviders = Array.from(new Set(models.map((m) => m.provider)));
const totalProvidersCount = uniqueProviders.length;
const modelsWithReviewedPricing = models.filter(
  (m) => m.apiPricing !== null && m.apiPricing !== undefined,
).length;
const reviewedProviders = Array.from(
  new Set(
    models
      .filter((m) => m.apiPricing !== null && m.apiPricing !== undefined)
      .map((m) => m.provider),
  ),
);
const reviewedProviderCount = reviewedProviders.length;
const isVerifiedEdition = models.some((m) => m.dataKind === 'verified');
---

<RootLayout title="How Synapse scores and recommends AI models | Methodology"
  ><div class="page-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">NOTHING BEHIND THE CURTAIN</span><h1>
      Understand the score.<br />Then make your own call.
    </h1><p>
      Numbers should make a decision clearer. Here’s what ours mean, where they
      come from, and where they stop.
    </p>
  </div>
  <article class="prose">
    <section id="sample-data">
      <h2>First, a note about this edition.</h2>
      {
        isVerifiedEdition ? (
          <>
            <p>
              This edition evaluates {totalModelsCount} production and frontier
              AI models across {totalProvidersCount} providers, sourced from
              authoritative public benchmarks including LiveBench, LMSYS Chatbot
              Arena, and provider-published technical specifications.
            </p>
            <p>
              Every score reflects normalized empirical evaluations or
              documented pricing structures. Where live or official data is
              unavailable, fields remain unverified or marked as estimates
              rather than populated with speculative figures.
            </p>
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <p>
              This local edition contains {totalModelsCount} illustrative model
              fixtures. Synthetic inputs demonstrate the calculation pipeline;
              they are not public benchmarks or internal evaluation runs.
              Evidence confidence is 0% because these fixtures have no real
              supporting measurements.
            </p>
          </>
        )
      }
      <p>
        Recommendations and cost estimates let you explore tradeoffs across
        standard workloads. Always verify current provider terms before making
        production commitments.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>One scale, different strengths.</h2><p>
        Capability scores range from 0 to 100. Each evidence record stores the
        raw measurement, its scale, its source, and its date. Normalization
        converts it to a common range:
      </p><p><code>100 × (raw − minimum) / (maximum − minimum)</code></p><p>
        Values are clamped to 0-100 and rounded to whole numbers. In this
        sample, each capability uses one synthetic input on a 0-1,000 scale.
        Real benchmarks need documented bounds, direction, task comparability,
        and validation before they can contribute. Normalization alone does not
        make incompatible tests comparable.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Overall quality. Not just the cheapest answer.</h2><p>
        The overall score is a weighted sum of the individual capabilities,
        rounded to a whole number. Price has only a 2% influence. Specialized
        rankings keep each capability visible independently.
      </p><div class="weight-grid">
        {
          Object.entries(overallWeights).map(([key, value]) => (
            <div>
              <>
                <span>{metricLabels[key as keyof typeof overallWeights]}</span>
                <strong>{Math.round(value * 100)}%</strong>
              </>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div><p>
        These are provisional product weights, version <code>sample-1.0</code>.
        They live in one configuration file and are exposed on model detail
        pages.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Cost efficiency</strong> in Synapse evaluates capability-adjusted
        economic value by mapping effective blended token pricing (accounting for
        cached input discounts across standard workload profiles) to an inverted logarithmic
        0–100 scale ($0.05/1M to $100/1M tokens):
      </p>
      <p class="micro muted">
        Effective Blended Price = Cached Rate × Cached Ratio + Input Rate ×
        Input Ratio + Output Rate × Output Ratio.<br />
        Score = 100 − ((log10(Effective Price) − log10(0.05)) / (log10(100) − log10(0.05)))
        × 100.
      </p>
      <p>
        This models pricing efficiency per token across prompt caching
        workloads, in contrast to LiveBench's independent "Cost / task" metric
        which evaluates execution token expenditure per successful benchmark
        problem.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Confidence is about evidence.</h2><p>
        Our confidence function combines independent source count (20%), source
        quality (20%), recency (15%), internal test count (15%), consistency
        (10%), and capability coverage (20%). Source count saturates at five,
        test count at 50, freshness decays over 180 days, and variance is scaled
        over 25 points.
      </p><p>
        This is an evidence-coverage heuristic, not a statistical probability
        that a score is correct. All fictional fixture scores have 0%
        confidence. No number of synthetic measurements can establish real-world
        confidence.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>A recommendation you can follow.</h2><p>
        The finder filters out models that do not fit your standard input API
        price limit or required vision support. It then combines the chosen task
        score (55%), your priority (30%), and reliability (15%). Quality uses
        intelligence; speed uses speed; cost and value use cost efficiency;
        balanced uses overall.
      </p><p>
        Ties are resolved by lower current input API price and then model slug.
        The best match has the highest fit score. The value choice has the
        highest cost-efficiency score among remaining eligible models. The
        alternative is the next available fit. No sponsorship changes these
        results.
      </p><p>
        Free requires verified zero input and output rates. Very cheap caps
        input at $1 / 1M tokens; moderate at $5 / 1M. Flexible applies no cap.
        Capped filters exclude missing, stale and tiered rates. Output charges
        are separate.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="api-pricing">
      <h2>Official API pricing first.</h2>
      <p>
        We use official provider pricing, then a verified OpenRouter fallback,
        otherwise unavailable. Each rate stores its value, currency, unit,
        source name and URL, retrieval date, and effective date when the
        provider supplies one. A missing rate is never treated as free.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reviewed standard text rates currently cover {modelsWithReviewedPricing} models
        across {reviewedProviderCount} official provider domains (including {
          reviewedProviders.slice(0, 4).join(', ')
        }{reviewedProviders.length > 4 ? ', and others' : ''}). Other pricing
        awaits verification. Historical ingestion prices are not used for
        published rates or estimates.
      </p>
      <p>
        Input, output and cached reads are distinct. Context tiers stay
        separate. Comparison sorting uses current single-tier rates; blended API
        price explicitly uses 70% input and 30% output. Neither determines a
        user's bill.
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculator starts empty. Total = requests * (uncached input * input
        rate + cached input * cache-read rate + output * output rate) /
        1,000,000, plus supported extras. Token counts are per request; cache
        storage is the total stored token-hours across the workload. All
        requests are assumed to have the user-entered counts. Retries must be
        counted explicitly. Billed thinking tokens belong in output; we never
        infer them from an effort setting or score.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rates older than seven days need verification and are blocked from
        estimates and price ranking. Retrieval dates are fixed at source review,
        never refreshed by a build. Check the provider's current page before
        purchasing. No monthly estimate is shown. Benchmark task cost stays
        unavailable until a documented workload, source and reproducible
        methodology exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current calculator scope is standard text tokens, cache reads, supported
        cache writes, cache storage and Anthropic web searches. Batch, flex,
        priority, fast mode, residency premiums, audio/image conversion,
        account-level allowances and other tools are not calculated. These
        require their own reviewed billing rules.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic pricing</a
        > · <a
          href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Google pricing</a
        > · <a
          href="https://openai.com/api/pricing/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI pricing</a
        > · <a
          href="https://api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">DeepSeek pricing</a
        > · <a href="/pricing">Compare rates</a> · <a href="/cost"
          >Calculate your workload</a
        >
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Facts, evidence, and estimates stay separate.</h2><p>
        Provider documentation is the intended authority for price, context,
        modalities, and availability. Public evaluations and controlled internal
        testing supply evidence. Derived scores refer back to those
        measurements. Cost estimates retain their workload assumptions.
      </p><p>
        The import validator requires source references, dates, score
        methodology, evidence coverage, bounded values, and unique model slugs.
        Verified imports cannot contain mock sources or synthetic evidence.
        Imports are archived as new snapshots; existing historical snapshots are
        not overwritten.
      </p><p>
        Structural validation cannot establish whether an external claim is
        true. A human source review is required before a verified import is
        selected for publication. Pricing reviews are documented separately from
        catalog and benchmark validation. Provider pages are reviewed snapshots,
        not a live billing feed.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Freshness is part of the answer.</h2><p>
        Model facts retain the source update date and last verification date;
        scores retain their calculation date; pricing retains its effective
        snapshot date. A fixture creation date never masquerades as provider
        verification. Old values remain in history rather than being silently
        replaced.
      </p><p>
        Use scores to narrow a choice, then review the evidence and test a model
        on your own work. Small score differences rarely tell the whole story.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</RootLayout>
